module dft/kmesh
  The k-points the SCF loop samples, and their weights, for an fcc crystal.

  The density is an average over the Brillouin zone: every k contributes its
  occupied bands. The integral is replaced by a sum over a regular mesh,
  `k = (i + s) / NKMESH` in units of the reciprocal vectors, for
  `i = 0 .. NKMESH-1` along each direction, and a shift s. The deck sets
  NKMESH; this file sets NKPT, KFRAC (each k as fractions of the reciprocal
  vectors) and KWT (weights that add up to one).

  The mesh uses four shifts, `(1/2 1/2 1/2)`, `(1/2 0 0)`, `(0 1/2 0)` and
  `(0 0 1/2)`, not one. The engine never symmetrizes the density, so the
  density has the crystal's symmetry only if the mesh does. A rotation of the
  crystal acts on these fractional coordinates as an integer matrix, and it
  sends the shift `(1/2 1/2 1/2)` to one of the other three. The four
  together are closed under every rotation, and silicon's valence-band top
  comes out as the triplet it should be
  [test "the valence-band top at Gamma is a TRIPLET: the density has the crystal's full symmetry"].

  Time reversal gives k and -k the same density, so each such pair is kept
  once with twice the weight: the loop looks for the partner of each new
  point among the points already kept. A point that is its own partner (k
  and -k differ by a reciprocal lattice vector) is kept with single weight.
  The run prints how many points are left as NKPT.

kwrap  @e4627950
  what  A fractional coordinate moved into the interval from minus one half
        (excluded) to one half (included), so that k and a copy of it shifted
        by a reciprocal lattice vector compare equal.
